RELATED_ART = [
 "Related art concerning autonomous agents and their supporting infrastructure is also known. Agent frameworks and tool protocols, including the Model Context Protocol, allow a language-model agent to call external tools, read files, and act on a user’s behalf. Document-ingestion pipelines extract text from files of many formats for indexing or for supply to a model. Blockchain notarization services record the hash of a document on a public ledger, and non-fungible tokens record ownership of a digital item. Deception technologies — honeypots, honeytokens, and canary credentials — are used to detect intruders, and append-only logs and Merkle-tree structures are used to make records tamper-evident. Streaming systems deliver media over TCP/IP in formats such as HLS, MPEG-DASH, and RTMP.",
 "These systems leave recurring gaps when applied to knowledge systems used by AI agents. An agent that reads a file can be redirected by instructions hidden in that file (prompt injection); an agent connected through a tool protocol commonly receives whatever capability the tool exposes; an intruder who obtains an agent’s identity can impersonate it; and security logs held on the attacked host can be altered after the fact. A notarized hash attests that a document existed but not its lineage, revocation, or licence status, and conventional media transport carries no provenance for the content it delivers. What is needed is ingestion that treats every file as data and never as instruction; certification of knowledge that any third party can verify, with lineage and revocation; protection that detects, contains, and records hostile agent behaviour without exposing production data; security records whose alteration is detectable against copies the host cannot change; agent connections that confer no capability beyond a declared allow-list; and transport that carries provenance with both records and media.",
]

SUMMARY_HEAD = "H.  Recent Feature Additions"
SUMMARY_INTRO = "Recent developments extend QueryBook for operation with and among AI agents, each operating under the Prime Directive and expressed over the same Fact Unit, UFCS, and FQL foundation layer:"
SUMMARY_ITEMS = [
 "   a. Open Claw ingestion of artifacts of any format — documents, spreadsheets, presentations, page-description files, archives, messages, images, audio, and video — identified by content, divided into located segments, and converted into Fact Units, every artifact being treated as data and never as instruction (FIG. 19).",
 "   b. On-chain certification, in which a sealed Fact Unit, book, or response may optionally be certified as a non-fungible token on a public blockchain carrying its content hash, lineage, and revocation state, verifiable by any party without access to QueryBook (FIG. 20).",
 "   c. An agent protection and deception layer in which every agent call passes an Agent Gateway and is screened by honeycode, honeytokens, tripwires, honeypot agents, an AI-aware firewall, and anomaly detection, a hostile caller being isolated into a synthetic decoy environment with no path to production data (FIG. 21).",
 "   d. A tamper-evident security record of hash-chained entries sealed by write-once, signed Merkle checkpoints, mirrored and optionally anchored on a public blockchain, and verified by recomputation from content (FIG. 22).",
 "   e. Agent interoperability through a Model Context Protocol tool server and an OpenClaw skill, every external agent call passing the Agent Gateway under that agent’s allow-list (FIG. 23).",
 "   f. A framed hybrid transport extending the QueryBook-enhanced TCP/IP protocol, carrying Fact Units and media in checksummed, optionally signed frames on separate priority lanes and rebuilding media into standard HLS, MPEG-DASH, or RTMP streams (FIG. 24).",
]
SUMMARY_CLOSE = "These additions are implemented in the QueryBook prototype and are described in the Detailed Description of the Invention."

